{"name":"Snow Drift","hex":"#e3e3dc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"snow drift","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of snowdrift."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snow_drift"}],"entries":[{"title":"Snowdrift","blocks":[{"text":"A snowdrift is a deposit of snow sculpted by wind into a mound during a snowstorm. Snowdrifts resemble sand dunes and are formed in a similar manner, namely, by wind moving light snow and depositing it when the wind has virtually stopped, usually against a stationary object. Snow normally crests and slopes off toward the surface on the windward side of a large object. On the leeward side, areas near the object are a bit lower than surrounding areas but are generally flatter."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/2024-01-21_07_06_48_Snow_drift_along_Burlington_County_Routes_630_and_637_%28Irick_Road%29_near_Woodlane_Road_in_Westampton_Township%2C_Burlington_County%2C_New_Jersey.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowdrift"},{"title":"Snow","blocks":[{"text":"Snow is a form of solid atmospheric precipitation consisting of individual ice crystals that grow while suspended in the air—usually within clouds—and then fall, accumulating on the ground, where they undergo further changes. It consists of frozen crystalline water throughout its life cycle, starting when, under suitable conditions, the ice crystals form in the atmosphere, increase to millimeter size, precipitate and accumulate on surfaces, then metamorphose in place, and ultimately melt, slide, or sublimate away."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg/330px-CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snow"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Long_Mynd_snowdrift.jpeg/250px-Long_Mynd_snowdrift.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Long_Mynd_snowdrift.jpeg/500px-Long_Mynd_snowdrift.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Long_Mynd_snowdrift.jpeg/250px-Long_Mynd_snowdrift.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALong_Mynd_snowdrift.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Tall_Snow_Drift_in_Kenosha%2C_Wisconsin.jpg/250px-Tall_Snow_Drift_in_Kenosha%2C_Wisconsin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Tall_Snow_Drift_in_Kenosha%2C_Wisconsin.jpg/500px-Tall_Snow_Drift_in_Kenosha%2C_Wisconsin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Tall_Snow_Drift_in_Kenosha%2C_Wisconsin.jpg/250px-Tall_Snow_Drift